    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-275-supplement",
    "abstract": "Implementation of the implicit version of MSPACMAMMSPACMAM (Dinauer et al., 2022) calculates vertical profiles of organic (POC), calcite, aragonite, and opal (OPAL) particles through depth-by-depth temporal integration of particle losses and downward fluxes until a dynamic equilibrium is achieved.This approach requires much shorter time steps than those of Bern3D (3 days), making its direct implementation costly.To reduce computational cost and increase numerical stability, we devised an implicit scheme which estimates the dynamic equilibrium based on primary production, particle sinking speed and environmental conditions in the water column.Our model operates on a 3D grid with and indicating longitude and latitude of the center of a grid cell. indicates the depth of the upper boundary of the grid cell , and +1 indicates the lower boundary of the grid cell .is the thickness of cell .In the following , are omitted to ease notation.Variables are specific for depth (), size class () and particle component (; POC,calcite,aragonite,opal): 0(0, , ): export flux out of euphotic zone (mol/(m 2 /s)) (, ): mean sinking speed in box (m/s) (approximated by ( , )) (, ) = ()/(, ) : average time particle is spending in box (s) (approximated by ()/( , ) ( , , ): particle remineralization/dissolution rate (1/s) at depth : model time step (s)The 1-dimensional equation for the divergence of a particle flux can be written as + = 0, with particle concentration , particle velocity , and particle flux = .For particles dissolving with rate , the temporal change in particle concentration is:Where / is the time, , the particles require for travelling distance .We get = and this equation is transferred to our model grid.We can then calculate the degradation rate in box k between depth k' and k'+1 either relative to the input f(k',l,m), or output f(k'+1,l,m), of the box, or a mix of the two, resulting in three different approaches to derive f(k'+1,l,m) from f(k',l,m):",
    "field": "Marine carbon cycle modeling",
    "problem": "The direct implementation of the MSPACMAM particle flux model is computationally costly and numerically unstable for climate models because it requires much shorter time steps, necessitating the development of a more efficient implicit scheme.",
    "method": "To reduce the computational cost and instability of the explicit MSPACMAM model, this paper proposes a new implicit scheme that estimates the dynamic equilibrium of vertical particle fluxes based on primary production, sinking speed, and environmental conditions in the water column.",
    "task": "The task is to implement a computationally efficient implicit scheme for a particle flux model and an updated iron cycle into a climate model to improve simulations of marine carbon cycle dynamics.",
